Chapter: X509Chain
  cites Encode chapter X509
  cites Encode chapter Asn1
  cites Foreword chapter Ed25519
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The chain walk. X509 said what a certificate IS; this chapter says whether
 to believe it. The two are kept apart on purpose: a parser that also
 decides trust tends to decide it by accident, in a branch nobody meant as
 a policy.

 A verdict is a variant and never a Boolean. "False" tells a caller that
 something is wrong and nothing about what, which means the caller cannot
 log it, cannot alert on it, and cannot tell an expired certificate (a
 clock problem, probably benign) from a bad signature (an attack, never
 benign). Every rejection below names itself.

 This chapter has NO CLOCK. The current time is a parameter, because a
 library that reaches for a clock cannot be tested at an interesting
 instant and cannot run where there is no clock -- and on bare metal there
 often is not one. The caller supplies the time; the caller is where the
 clock lives.

 What a chain walk must get right, and what each rule is here to stop:

 The issuer link is compared as RAW DER BYTES, never as decoded text. Two
 names that print the same can be different encodings, and a decoder that
 normalises them is a decoder that can be talked into believing one
 authority is another.

 An issuer must be a CA. A leaf certificate that is allowed to sign
 another certificate is an authority, and every certificate it signs is
 trusted. This is the single most valuable bit in the whole structure, and
 it is one bit.

 An issuer's key must be able to VERIFY. An X25519 key agrees keys; it
 signs nothing. X509 makes that a type, and this chapter honours it.

 To x509-chain-verify gives X509Verdict, deciding whether a certificate chain is trustworthy at a given time.
 To x509-time-norm gives Maybe (List Integer), normalising a certificate time to 14 comparable digits.

 We say:

Section: Verdict

  X509Verdict =
    | ChainOk
    | ChainEmpty
    | ChainNotYetValid
    | ChainExpired
    | ChainBadTime
    | ChainBadSignature
    | ChainBrokenIssuer
    | ChainIssuerNotCa
    | ChainIssuerCannotSign
    | ChainNoAnchor
    | ChainTooLong

 A trust anchor is a name and a key. It is not a certificate: an anchor is
 trusted because the operator put it there, not because something signed
 it, and modelling it as a certificate invites somebody to go looking for
 its issuer.

  X509Anchor = record {
    xa-subject : List Integer,
    xa-key : X509Key
  }

 A chain longer than this is refused rather than walked. The bound is not
 a performance concern -- it is that an unbounded walk over attacker-
 supplied certificates is an attacker-supplied loop.

  x509-max-chain : Integer = 8

Section: Time

 A certificate time is either a UTCTime (YYMMDDHHMMSSZ, 13 bytes) or a
 GeneralizedTime (YYYYMMDDHHMMSSZ, 15 bytes). Both normalise to fourteen
 ASCII digits, which compare correctly as bytes because every field is
 fixed width and zero padded.

 RFC 5280 section 4.1.2.5.1: in a UTCTime, a two-digit year of 50 or more
 means 19xx and anything less means 20xx. Getting this backwards makes a
 certificate from 1996 look like one that expires in 2096.

  x509-time-norm : List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  x509-time-norm (t) =
    if list-length t == 13 then x509-utc-time t
    else if list-length t == 15 then x509-gen-time t
    else None

  x509-ascii-z : Integer = 90
  x509-ascii-0 : Integer = 48
  x509-ascii-9 : Integer = 57

  x509-utc-time : List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  x509-utc-time (t) =
    if list-at t 12 /= x509-ascii-z then None
    else if x509-all-digits t 0 12 == False then None
    else Just (x509-century (list-at t 0) & x509-take t 0 12)

 The century bytes, as ASCII: "19" or "20".

  x509-century : Integer -> List Integer
  x509-century (y0) =
    if y0 >= x509-ascii-0 + 5 then [49, 57] else [50, 48]

  x509-gen-time : List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  x509-gen-time (t) =
    if list-at t 14 /= x509-ascii-z then None
    else if x509-all-digits t 0 14 == False then None
    else Just (x509-take t 0 14)

 A time field that is not digits is not a time. A parser that shrugs at
 this is a parser that will compare garbage and get an answer.

  x509-all-digits : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  x509-all-digits (t) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then True
    else if list-at t i < x509-ascii-0 then False
    else if list-at t i > x509-ascii-9 then False
    else x509-all-digits t (i + 1) n

  x509-take : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  x509-take (t) (off) (n) = x509-take-loop t off n 0 []

  x509-take-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  x509-take-loop (t) (off) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else if off + i >= list-length t then acc
    else x509-take-loop t off n (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at t (off + i)))

 Lexicographic, over equal fixed widths, which is numeric order.

  x509-time-le : List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  x509-time-le (a) (b) = x509-le-loop a b 0

  x509-le-loop : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  x509-le-loop (a) (b) (i) =
    if i >= list-length a then True
    else if i >= list-length b then False
    else if list-at a i < list-at b i then True
    else if list-at a i > list-at b i then False
    else x509-le-loop a b (i + 1)

 notBefore <= now <= notAfter. Both ends, and the failures are told apart:
 a certificate that is not valid yet is usually a clock that is wrong, and
 one that has expired is usually a certificate that has expired.

  x509-valid-at : X509Cert, List Integer -> X509Verdict
  x509-valid-at (c) (now) =
    when x509-time-norm (c.xc-not-before)
      is None -> ChainBadTime
      is Just (nb) -> x509-valid-after c now nb

  x509-valid-after : X509Cert, List Integer, List Integer -> X509Verdict
  x509-valid-after (c) (now) (nb) =
    when x509-time-norm (c.xc-not-after)
      is None -> ChainBadTime
      is Just (na) -> x509-window nb na now

  x509-window : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> X509Verdict
  x509-window (nb) (na) (now) =
    if x509-time-le nb now == False then ChainNotYetValid
    else if x509-time-le now na == False then ChainExpired
    else ChainOk

Section: Signature

 A certificate is signed over its tbsCertificate -- the bytes the parser
 kept, not a re-encoding of them. The key must be one that can sign at all.

  x509-signed-by : X509Cert, X509Key -> Boolean
  x509-signed-by (c) (k) =
    if x509-can-verify k == False then False
    else ed25519-verify (k.xk-bytes) (c.xc-tbs) (c.xc-sig)

 An issuer must be a CA and must hold a signing key. Both, and the two
 failures are named separately because they are different mistakes.

  x509-issuer-ok : X509Cert -> X509Verdict
  x509-issuer-ok (issuer) =
    if issuer.xc-is-ca == False then ChainIssuerNotCa
    else if x509-can-verify (issuer.xc-key) == False then ChainIssuerCannotSign
    else ChainOk

Section: Names

  x509-name-eq : List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  x509-name-eq (a) (b) =
    if list-length a /= list-length b then False
    else x509-name-loop a b 0 (list-length a)

  x509-name-loop : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  x509-name-loop (a) (b) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then True
    else if list-at a i /= list-at b i then False
    else x509-name-loop a b (i + 1) n

Section: The Walk

 The chain is leaf first. Each certificate is checked against the next one
 up; the last one is checked against a trust anchor. A chain that reaches
 no anchor is refused -- there is no "well, it looked fine" ending.

  x509-chain-verify : List X509Cert, List X509Anchor, List Integer -> X509Verdict
  x509-chain-verify (chain) (anchors) (now) =
    if list-length chain < 1 then ChainEmpty
    else if list-length chain > x509-max-chain then ChainTooLong
    else x509-walk chain anchors now 0

  x509-walk : List X509Cert, List X509Anchor, List Integer, Integer -> X509Verdict
  x509-walk (chain) (anchors) (now) (i) =
    when x509-valid-at (list-at chain i) now
      is ChainOk -> x509-walk-link chain anchors now i
      is otherwise -> x509-valid-at (list-at chain i) now

 The last certificate in the chain must be vouched for by an anchor.
 Everything before it must be vouched for by the certificate above it.

  x509-walk-link : List X509Cert, List X509Anchor, List Integer, Integer -> X509Verdict
  x509-walk-link (chain) (anchors) (now) (i) =
    if i == list-length chain - 1 then x509-to-anchor (list-at chain i) anchors 0
    else x509-to-issuer chain anchors now i

  x509-to-issuer : List X509Cert, List X509Anchor, List Integer, Integer -> X509Verdict
  x509-to-issuer (chain) (anchors) (now) (i) =
    when x509-issuer-ok (list-at chain (i + 1))
      is ChainOk -> x509-issuer-link chain anchors now i
      is otherwise -> x509-issuer-ok (list-at chain (i + 1))

  x509-issuer-link : List X509Cert, List X509Anchor, List Integer, Integer -> X509Verdict
  x509-issuer-link (chain) (anchors) (now) (i) =
    if x509-name-eq ((list-at chain i).xc-issuer) ((list-at chain (i + 1)).xc-subject) == False
    then ChainBrokenIssuer
    else if x509-signed-by (list-at chain i) ((list-at chain (i + 1)).xc-key) == False
    then ChainBadSignature
    else x509-walk chain anchors now (i + 1)

 An anchor matches on its name, and then it must actually have signed the
 thing. A name match alone proves nothing at all -- anyone can put any name
 in an issuer field.

  x509-to-anchor : X509Cert, List X509Anchor, Integer -> X509Verdict
  x509-to-anchor (c) (anchors) (i) =
    if i >= list-length anchors then ChainNoAnchor
    else if x509-name-eq (c.xc-issuer) ((list-at anchors i).xa-subject) == False
    then x509-to-anchor c anchors (i + 1)
    else x509-anchor-sig c anchors i

 The name matched. If the signature does not, we do NOT go looking for
 another anchor with the same name -- that is an oracle, and it lets an
 attacker who can register a colliding name grind against every key we
 hold. One anchor, one answer.

  x509-anchor-sig : X509Cert, List X509Anchor, Integer -> X509Verdict
  x509-anchor-sig (c) (anchors) (i) =
    if x509-signed-by c ((list-at anchors i).xa-key) then ChainOk
    else ChainBadSignature
